    14	# Design analysis for the fused variant:
    15	# - Concrete trace: in the base task, two nearby dents from opposite host regions
    16	#   stamp opposite-color 3x3 patches, and the overlap color is just a write-order
    17	#   artifact. Here the same dents first generate two 3x3 footprints, those
    18	#   footprints are unioned into one overlap-connected patch component, the
    19	#   component centroid is computed, and the dent center closest to that centroid
    20	#   chooses the color for the entire merged component. Only after this merge do we
    21	#   crop the final answer to the patch bbox expanded by one cell.
    22	# - Complexity calibration: the generator builds one compact mixed-color cluster
    23	#   with 5-8 dents. That keeps the crop visually small, but the cluster is large
    24	#   enough that merge-vs-naive stamping changes multiple cells and is easy to see.
    25	# - Hidden assumptions that no longer hold: `out = clean.copy()` is no longer the
    26	#   final frame because unchanged bulk regions must be discarded; dent spacing can
    27	#   no longer rely on the original separation heuristic because overlapped patches
    28	#   are now required; overlap color cannot depend on scan order.
    29	# - Placement legality: every selected dent stays in-bounds, every accepted set of
    30	#   dents is verified simultaneously against the cleanup+detection logic, and the
    31	#   chosen dents form a single overlap-connected component before noise is added.
    32	# - Visual observability: the generator requires at least one merged component that
    33	#   contains dents of both patch colors and differs nontrivially from naive
    34	#   last-write stamping. The crop ratio is also kept small so the D11/1 change is
    35	#   visible without needing many examples.
